WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:05.207
Picture a corner shop with an odd habit.

2
00:00:05.207 --> 00:00:09.041
Every evening, the shopkeeper packs the whole shop into boxes.

3
00:00:09.041 --> 00:00:18.071
Every morning, she unlocks the door, switches on the lights, unpacks each box, checks every item against its delivery note, and fills the shelves.

4
00:00:18.267 --> 00:00:20.772
Only then can she serve the first customer.

5
00:00:20.772 --> 00:00:24.851
And even then she's slow, because she still looks up where the tea is.

6
00:00:25.733 --> 00:00:28.834
Here's a question that's easy to be shy about.

7
00:00:28.834 --> 00:00:31.192
My main method is three lines long.

8
00:00:31.192 --> 00:00:37.528
So why does the JVM, the program that runs my compiled Java and Kotlin, take so long to start?

9
00:00:37.528 --> 00:00:41.504
And doesn't it remember anything from the last time it ran?

10
00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:47.253
Our running example is a small parcel tracking service.

11
00:00:47.253 --> 00:00:51.399
Ask it where parcel PX-1042 is, and it tells you.

12
00:00:51.399 --> 00:00:55.798
One run on JDK 25 printed: started in 0.151 seconds.

13
00:00:55.798 --> 00:01:01.805
Yet the first answer reached the outside 429 milliseconds after launch.

14
00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:03.396
Both numbers are true.

15
00:01:03.396 --> 00:01:05.109
They time different things.

16
00:01:05.109 --> 00:01:09.615
Let's find out what fills that wait, and we'll start by counting boxes.

17
00:01:10.533 --> 00:01:11.975
Here's a tiny demo.

18
00:01:11.975 --> 00:01:14.707
It has exactly one class of its own.

19
00:01:14.707 --> 00:01:20.323
It reads three lines of settings, formats a date, and joins a few strings.

20
00:01:20.323 --> 00:01:23.738
How many classes does the JVM load to run it?

21
00:01:23.738 --> 00:01:24.042
Ten?

22
00:01:24.042 --> 00:01:24.801
A hundred?

23
00:01:24.801 --> 00:01:25.863
Eight hundred?

24
00:01:25.863 --> 00:01:27.077
Pause and guess.

25
00:01:30.867 --> 00:01:33.777
On JDK 25, it loaded 818 classes.

26
00:01:33.777 --> 00:01:36.511
Here they are, one square each.

27
00:01:36.511 --> 00:01:39.244
Exactly one came from the demo.

28
00:01:39.244 --> 00:01:40.920
It's the green one.

29
00:01:41.067 --> 00:01:45.247
793 came ready made, from an archive the JDK ships.

30
00:01:45.247 --> 00:01:50.986
19 were read from the JDK's own modules file and unpacked on the spot.

31
00:01:50.986 --> 00:01:55.167
And 5 were made in memory, for things like lambdas.

32
00:01:55.333 --> 00:01:59.354
So we asked the shop for one box, and it unpacked 818.

33
00:01:59.354 --> 00:02:01.364
Ours is in there somewhere.

34
00:02:01.364 --> 00:02:03.225
Bring a magnifying glass.

35
00:02:03.400 --> 00:02:05.955
Jokes aside, that's the point.

36
00:02:05.955 --> 00:02:09.616
Most of the work is the JDK, not your code.

37
00:02:09.616 --> 00:02:18.473
A generated app with 300 classes of its own loaded 1,537 classes on JDK 21, with the archive turned off.

38
00:02:18.473 --> 00:02:21.368
Its own classes were only a fifth.

39
00:02:22.267 --> 00:02:25.055
So what does the JVM do with all those boxes?

40
00:02:25.055 --> 00:02:30.259
Let's walk through the morning, one step at a time, and match each step to the shop.

41
00:02:30.400 --> 00:02:35.284
When you type java, a small native program called the launcher starts first.

42
00:02:35.284 --> 00:02:39.332
Native means compiled for your machine, the way C programs are.

43
00:02:39.332 --> 00:02:44.023
The launcher loads the JVM and asks it to create a VM, a virtual machine.

44
00:02:44.023 --> 00:02:47.493
That's unlocking the door and switching on the lights.

45
00:02:47.667 --> 00:02:49.528
Creating the VM is real work.

46
00:02:49.528 --> 00:02:54.406
The JVM reserves memory for the heap, the area where your objects will live.

47
00:02:54.406 --> 00:03:01.403
It builds its interpreter, starts its own helper threads, and sets up the core classes and the module system.

48
00:03:01.403 --> 00:03:04.163
Only then does it look for your main class.

49
00:03:04.333 --> 00:03:05.419
Then the boxes.

50
00:03:05.419 --> 00:03:16.064
The first time something needs a class, the JVM finds its class file and parses it into its own form, in metaspace, memory it keeps for class data.

51
00:03:16.064 --> 00:03:17.729
That's unpacking a box.

52
00:03:17.867 --> 00:03:23.765
Next it verifies the bytecode, the instructions javac and kotlinc write into class files.

53
00:03:23.765 --> 00:03:28.404
It checks that every instruction is type safe before any of them runs.

54
00:03:28.404 --> 00:03:31.519
That's the delivery note, checked item by item.

55
00:03:31.667 --> 00:03:39.401
Then your code runs in the interpreter, one instruction at a time, like a shopkeeper looking up every price in the catalogue.

56
00:03:39.401 --> 00:03:44.289
The JIT compilers turn hot methods, the ones that run often, into machine code.

57
00:03:44.289 --> 00:03:48.621
But first they wait for profiles: counts of what the code really does.

58
00:03:48.621 --> 00:03:52.643
That's learning the popular items by heart, and it takes a while.

59
00:03:52.800 --> 00:03:59.067
And a framework, a library that starts and wires your program for you, adds chores of its own.

60
00:03:59.067 --> 00:04:04.867
It scans classes for annotations, creates objects by name, and reads its configuration.

61
00:04:05.067 --> 00:04:07.070
Now the second shy question.

62
00:04:07.070 --> 00:04:09.359
Stop the JVM and start it again.

63
00:04:09.359 --> 00:04:12.077
How much of this morning does it skip?

64
00:04:12.077 --> 00:04:13.221
Pause and guess.

65
00:04:16.467 --> 00:04:17.250
Almost none.

66
00:04:17.250 --> 00:04:19.665
Nothing of this survives the process.

67
00:04:19.665 --> 00:04:25.148
The next launch unpacks, checks and learns it all again, with exactly the same care.

68
00:04:25.333 --> 00:04:27.675
You have to admire the dedication.

69
00:04:27.675 --> 00:04:33.392
The same tins of tea, checked against the same delivery note, every single morning.

70
00:04:33.392 --> 00:04:34.357
Thorough, yes.

71
00:04:34.357 --> 00:04:35.045
Quick, no.

72
00:04:35.200 --> 00:04:37.237
So the point really is this.

73
00:04:37.237 --> 00:04:40.803
Start-up is work the JVM repeats on every launch.

74
00:04:41.000 --> 00:04:44.215
There is one exception, and we already saw it.

75
00:04:44.215 --> 00:04:49.738
A JDK build ships an archive of about 1,300 of its own classes, already parsed.

76
00:04:49.738 --> 00:04:53.512
The JVM maps them from disk instead of unpacking them.

77
00:04:53.512 --> 00:04:55.749
Those were the 793 blue squares.

78
00:04:55.749 --> 00:05:00.852
The next lesson is about that archive, and the bigger ones you can build.

79
00:05:01.733 --> 00:05:04.060
You can watch the unpacking yourself.

80
00:05:04.060 --> 00:05:08.147
Run java with -Xlog:class+load, and it prints one line per class.

81
00:05:08.147 --> 00:05:11.417
The end of each line says where the class came from.

82
00:05:11.600 --> 00:05:15.441
shared objects file means ready made, from the archive.

83
00:05:15.441 --> 00:05:19.143
jrt means the JDK's modules file, parsed on the spot.

84
00:05:19.143 --> 00:05:25.358
file is your own class path, the folders and JAR files where the JVM looks for your code.

85
00:05:25.358 --> 00:05:31.713
And a class name as the source marks a hidden class, made in memory while the program runs.

86
00:05:31.713 --> 00:05:33.180
Each lambda gets one.

87
00:05:34.067 --> 00:05:37.440
Here the shop picture breaks, and the break matters.

88
00:05:37.440 --> 00:05:40.749
Our shopkeeper unpacks everything before she opens.

89
00:05:40.749 --> 00:05:41.787
The JVM doesn't.

90
00:05:41.787 --> 00:05:47.043
It opens first, and unpacks a box only when someone asks for something inside it.

91
00:05:47.200 --> 00:05:49.375
That's called lazy loading.

92
00:05:49.375 --> 00:05:55.576
It's why a one class demo loads 818 classes, and not every class the JDK has.

93
00:05:56.467 --> 00:05:58.434
Three things to keep in mind.

94
00:05:58.434 --> 00:06:02.232
One: the line your program prints isn't the whole story.

95
00:06:02.232 --> 00:06:03.995
The wait starts at launch.

96
00:06:03.995 --> 00:06:05.759
Two: count before you cut.

97
00:06:05.759 --> 00:06:10.914
The class load log shows whose classes they are, and most will be the JDK's.

98
00:06:10.914 --> 00:06:14.508
Three: the archive covers only the JDK's own classes.

99
00:06:14.508 --> 00:06:18.849
Your classes, the interpreter and the JIT start cold every time.

100
00:06:19.733 --> 00:06:22.706
So, why does a three line main start slowly?

101
00:06:22.706 --> 00:06:26.220
Because every launch runs the whole morning routine.

102
00:06:26.220 --> 00:06:27.166
Open the shop.

103
00:06:27.166 --> 00:06:30.747
Unpack and check each box the first time it's needed.

104
00:06:30.747 --> 00:06:33.517
Look up prices until the JIT learns them.

105
00:06:33.517 --> 00:06:35.679
Let the framework do its chores.

106
00:06:35.679 --> 00:06:41.220
And no, the JVM remembers almost nothing, apart from the JDK's ready made shelves.

107
00:06:41.400 --> 00:06:42.189
Quick check.

108
00:06:42.189 --> 00:06:44.357
A hidden class has no class file.

109
00:06:44.357 --> 00:06:47.052
Why can its source never start with file?

110
00:06:50.267 --> 00:06:53.858
Because file names the place a class's bytes were read from.

111
00:06:53.858 --> 00:06:56.431
A hidden class's bytes were made in memory.

112
00:06:56.431 --> 00:06:58.287
There was never a file to name.
